   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 297 of the laws of 2016, amending the alcoholic beverage control law and the executive law relating to a reorganization of the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to eliminating the expiration and repeal of certain provisions authorizing seven day license holders to sell gift bags and authorizing manufacturers to transport alcoholic beverages to an adjacent retail premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S5962",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5962-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 39,
       "fullName": "William J. Larkin Jr.",
       "imgName": "374_william_j._larkin_jr.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 374,
       "sessionMemberId": 1012,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "LARKIN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §18, Chap 297 of 2016",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5962       Revised 11/30/17\n\nSPONSOR: MURPHY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 297 of the laws of 2016,\namending the alcoholic beverage control law and the executive law relat-\ning to a reorganization of the alcoholic beverage control law, in\nrelation to eliminating the expiration and repeal of certain provisions\nauthorizing seven day license holders to sell gift bags and authorizing\nmanufacturers to transport alcoholic beverages to an adjacent retail\npremises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would make permanent certain provisions of the alcoholic\nbeverage control law authorizing the sale of gift bags and wrapping, and\nmanufacturers to transport alcoholic beverages to their retail estab-\nlishments.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one would amend section 18 of chapter 297 of the laws of 2016 to\nremove the sunset date on the provisions contained in sections six and\nthirteen of such act.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChapter 297, laws of 2016 included a provision to allow liquor stores to\nsell gift bags for profit and to authorize grocers to transport deliv-\neries of sealed alcoholic beverages across their supermarket to the\nrestaurant facilities.\n\nThe intent of these provisions was to allow liquor stores to make a\nprofit on items they were currently giving to customers and to allow\nsupermarkets more flexibility in where they locate their restaurants in\nthat an outside entrance would no longer be required. Both of these\nprovisions are set to sunset on November 6, 2019.\n\nThe sunset provisions are causing problems for both package stores and\nfood stores. The liquor stores, most of whom are small businesses, are\nnot willing to invest in inventory they may not be authorized to sell as\nof November 6, 2019. Furthermore, food stores cannot afford to pay\nconstruction costs to locate a restaurant or tavern within their store\nof the sunset provisions will prevent delivers in two years. This uncer-\ntainty is preventing both classes of licensees from taking full advan-\ntage of the new law.\n\nThis piece of legislation merely removes the sunset provisions and\nimplements the changes that were unanimously recommended by the ABC\nWorking Group formed by Governor Cuomo in 2016 to modernize the ABC law.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017: Passed Senate\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
